  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2+3\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\text{i}=\\frac{p+q\\text{i}}{2+3\\text{i}} \\\\ (6-\\text{i})(2+3\\text{i}) & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ 12+18\\text{i}-2\\text{i}-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ 12+3+16\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ 15+16\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n15=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=15$ and $q=16.$",
